WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:09.840
DAVID FEUER: Hello all, and
welcome to API 302, GraphQL:

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.670
Building a Consistent
Approach for the API Consumer.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:15.250
My name is David Feuer,
the head of global platform

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:18.430
strategy at Apigee, a part
of Google Cloud platform.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:20.530
And I'm joined today
by Miguel Mendoza,

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:24.530
a lead architect at Apigee, a
part of Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:26.270
Today we're going
to go over what

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.850
is GraphQL, what it's
good for, and what

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:30.440
are some of the
expected features that

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.180
are missing from GraphQL.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.370
Secondly, we're going
to go over consistency.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.470
What's the point of
consistency, and walk

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.540
through REST best
practices, and then

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:40.610
translating that to GraphQL.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:42.440
Lastly Miguel
Mendoza's going to do

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.660
a demo for us, of how
we would implement

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.220
some of this on top of Apigee.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.980
The first thing I'd like to
say is that GraphQL is awesome.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.380
GraphQL has some
terrific benefits.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.750
And if I had to break down
some of these benefits,

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:55.790
I would break them down
to three key areas.

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:58.450
The fact that there's a single
endpoint, so all the data

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.480
is exchanged [INAUDIBLE]
a single endpoint.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.140
So granularity for data
load and execution requests

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.630
in parallel, query
planning, all of that

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:07.610
is super straightforward
in GraphQL.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.670
The second is an incredibly
powerful query language

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.610
that prevents over fetching
and under fetching of data,

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:15.080
as opposed to,
let's say, in REST,

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.090
where many of the requests
have to be sequential.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.430
And then lastly, GraphQL
client libraries.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.760
So GraphQL is no
longer only the domain

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.200
of the ReactJS UI framework.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.920
But it's also available
for other client libraries

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.820
and for other languages
and paradigms.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.860
So that GraphQL is truly useful
to most developers today.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.930
So why isn't GraphQL everywhere?

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:38.450
I think ultimately, this
comes down to a bifurcation

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:39.810
that we're seeing in the market.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.430
The first is that developer
programs are frequently

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.500
separated from API programs.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.290
Developer programs
tend to be external

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:51.260
facing or internal facing
between business units,

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:54.410
so that the author of the
server and client are different.

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.630
In APIs, frequently
what we see is

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:58.430
that the author of
the client and server

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.380
are a tight-knit group of
people, or the same person.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.720
And that's why we're
seeing more of GraphQL.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.720
I think part of that has
to do with consistency.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.460
We'll talk about that today.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.310
But fundamentally,
self-service developer programs

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.432
are a growth strategy.

00:02:11.432 --> 00:02:12.890
And so developer
programs are a way

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.200
of getting internal partner and
external developers leveraging

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.700
your APIs, as opposed to API
programs, which are typically

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.850
an infrastructure strategy
driving interoperability

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:26.070
and decomposition of your
internal infrastructure.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.620
So what are the benefits of
GraphQL, why use GraphQL?

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.200
Well, we talked a little bit
about some of the differences.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.180
Let's do a little
bit of a deep dive.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:36.320
REST has multiple different
endpoints, and the URL taxonomy

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.540
that's used as a
logical resource manager

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.610
for different types of APIs.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.010
In GraphQL, there's
really one endpoint.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.530
And longer and larger
queries for customer data

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.740
encapsulate all the different
value and different value

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.240
propositions that
you would normally

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.960
see in those different
URLs in REST.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.610
In interactions,
REST uses HTTP VERBs,

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:59.450
and generally uses JSON XML in
order to exchange that data.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:02.390
In GraphQL, GraphQL uses
POST most of the time,

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.880
And also leverages
different query types

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.760
specified inside the
protocol, not in HTTP,

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.300
so there's no VERBs there, just
a custom query language called

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.080
Schema Definition Language.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.950
So even though it's a custom
query language on the request,

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:18.950
JSON is returned, which makes
it much easier for clients.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:20.450
But there's a
significant difference

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.053
in the interaction mechanism.

00:03:22.053 --> 00:03:23.720
Documentation is
different, whether it's

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.150
OpenAPI specs and
portals on the REST side,

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:29.870
really in GraphQL, there's a
schema generated documentation.

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:31.280
Frequently, it
will see customers

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:33.470
using Graphical, or
GraphQL Playground,

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:38.120
in order to interact with
schema based GraphQL endpoints.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.310
And then of course from
a discovery perspective,

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:42.290
the portal is the
Playground in REST

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.210
where a customer
would normally go,

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:47.210
or a developer would normally
go, to discover and interact

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.970
with REST APIs in order to
understand how they work.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:52.460
In GraphQL, the
portal actually is

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.650
a built in Playground that
accommodates development.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.230
It's almost like a
built in IDE, and so it

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.360
allows you to explore
new queries on the fly,

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.010
and use things
like tab completion

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:03.830
in order to discover those
queries in order to learn

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:05.490
how to use them better.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:07.280
And so, we're not
here to talk about

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.440
whether you should
use GraphQL or REST,

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.807
our philosophy is that you
should use GraphQL and REST.

00:04:12.807 --> 00:04:14.390
And I think one of
the best ways to do

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:16.547
that is to take some of
our REST-based practices

00:04:16.547 --> 00:04:17.630
and apply them to GraphQL.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:20.515
This is something that
we've seen in the market.

00:04:20.515 --> 00:04:22.640
This is something that we
really see working really

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:23.940
well at a number of customers.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.670
So we wanted to
bring those to you,

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:29.180
so that you could leverage them
and understand what those are.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:33.530
The basis of this philosophy is
that APIs are digital products.

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:36.740
APIs are a way for an
enterprise to take its assets

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:38.880
and put it in the
hands of developers,

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.860
whether those developers are
internal employees, partners,

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:45.770
or external customers, in
order to grow the company's

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.270
leverage of those assets.

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:49.670
Because APIs are
digital products,

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:52.790
we rely on developers having a
consistent experience in order

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:55.220
to understand how to use
those digital products,

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:58.280
and build the compelling
experiences they need in order

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.660
to bring your assets to market.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.170
And so consistency is
incredibly important

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:07.870
because developer friction
is a huge challenge

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:10.850
in adoption of APIs,
and in growth strategies

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:13.250
of digital companies.

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:17.180
Consistency in
GraphQL really starts

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:18.930
with a well-formed GraphQL.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:20.660
And I think we can
learn a lot about that

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:22.440
from well-formed REST.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.460
You would never expect
a well-formed REST query

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.470
to be list employees
by department.

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:29.130
That sounds like
a Java function.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:32.500
A well-formed REST query
would be a resource.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:35.200
Get employees, and
you pass employees ID.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.870
And so that is what we
consider well-formed REST.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:41.020
Nouns, And those
nouns are plural.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:44.440
Graph hierarchy is fundamentally
a data-driven hierarchy, right?

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:48.520
So why do we frequently treat
it like a functional hierarchy?

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.770
Well-formed GraphQL shouldn't
look like the Java functions,

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:52.900
which it frequently does.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.960
Well-formed GraphQL should
look like well-formed REST.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:59.410
Why not use books, and
titles, and authors,

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:02.012
and treat this graphical
hierarchy the way

00:06:02.012 --> 00:06:03.970
it should be treated, as
opposed to treating it

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:06.050
as a functional hierarchy.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:08.320
And so the first
recommendation we have

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:10.140
is that you should
be data-oriented.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:11.740
A graph hierarchy
is fundamentally

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.150
a data-driven hierarchy.

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:14.410
Be data-driven.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:15.790
Treat it that way.

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:18.960
Don't be so function oriented.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.150
The second recommendation
has to do with submitting

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:22.320
and retrieving data.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.750
Those are not always
mirror operations.

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:28.290
We frequently see in REST,
leverage of CQRS, right?

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:30.240
We see retrieving data
from multiple services,

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.130
and a microservice
architecture, being

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.130
completely separate from
how to submit that data.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:38.160
And so, as we think about
how to do this in GraphQL,

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.200
we specifically
think that mutations,

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.960
the way a GraphQL
developer submits data,

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.510
can get very complex
very quickly, especially

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.220
when there's a lot
of different types,

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:50.850
and especially when
submitting very little data.

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:52.740
Don't be afraid of CQRS.

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:55.080
Many of you have heard of
CQRS, which is Command Query

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:56.910
Responsibility Segregation.

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.410
It's a design pattern
that was first

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.450
identified by Martin Fowler.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:02.730
And fundamentally, it's
just a different model

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:05.850
to update information than the
model used to read information.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.470
And so we're seeing
in GraphQL programs,

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:11.490
especially in large enterprises,
especially those enterprises

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:13.590
that already have
REST, is that you

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:16.260
get the advantage of using
a REST-based frontend

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:18.540
by retrieving the
data with GraphQL,

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:21.840
but leveraging your existing
REST APIs in order to submit

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.260
that data to your backend.

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:27.210
The third pattern I'd
like to talk about

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:29.510
is optimizing for re-usability.

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:32.700
In REST, we think a lot about
our URL taxonomy, pagination,

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:34.890
filtering, and
creating consistency,

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:37.350
because developers can then
predict what the behavior will

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:38.700
be of an API call.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:41.640
A developer sees the
REST API and goes, a-ha,

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:43.610
I know how to
interact with this.

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:46.890
Well, GraphQL, we should
be doing the same thing.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:50.100
The challenge in large
enterprise GraphQL deployments

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:52.890
is that when it comes to
schema stitching, frequently,

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:55.680
different business units,
different lines of business,

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.720
are developing different
aspects of the schema,

00:07:57.720 --> 00:07:59.428
and then stitching
that together in order

00:07:59.428 --> 00:08:02.580
to look like one comprehensive
representation, one

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:05.250
big graph to hand to
an external developer.

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:07.620
The behavior of the
JSON returned though,

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.200
is frequently different,
because there is no consistency.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:12.330
It wasn't built
as an API product

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:13.650
as part of a developer program.

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:15.870
It was built as an
internal API, sort

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.670
of a functional way of
interacting between components.

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:21.720
So when we think about relay
cursor connections and input

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.390
hints, all the uniform
behavior that you

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:26.340
would expect in a
REST API should also

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.270
exist in the GraphQL API.

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:31.290
So in GraphQL, we should be
very careful about optimizing

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:33.900
for re-usability, and
avoiding situations

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:36.270
where the developer is
confused, or otherwise

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:38.490
doesn't understand
why the behavior is

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:40.922
different in one
part of the schema

00:08:40.922 --> 00:08:42.630
than it is in another
part of the schema.

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:44.970
And that's particularly
a problem with mutations,

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:48.423
because if you submit data to
one part of the schema one way,

00:08:48.423 --> 00:08:50.340
and it gets recorded one
way, you don't always

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:51.775
realize that when
you submitted it

00:08:51.775 --> 00:08:53.340
in another part
of the schema, it

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:54.930
got recorded another other way.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:58.360
So mutations are even a larger--

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.390
a larger surface
area of challenges

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:03.930
when it comes to optimizing
for re-usability and API

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:04.630
productization.

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:07.200
And so be particularly
afraid there.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.400
Be particularly conscientious
of the way you develop

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:14.520
and design mutations in GraphQL.

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.760
Lastly, I want to talk
about field names,

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:19.260
because this is something that
Miguel and I have experienced

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:20.220
personally.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:23.460
It is developer hostile
to provide different data

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:26.220
and behavior for field
names with the same name

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:27.870
in different parts
of the schema.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:31.200
So if there is a name field
in one part of the schema that

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.063
accepts first,
middle, last, name,

00:09:33.063 --> 00:09:35.730
and then there's a name field in
another part of the schema then

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.370
expects last name
dot first name, that

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:39.757
is developer hostile.

00:09:39.757 --> 00:09:42.090
You are not treating your
developers the way they expect

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:44.940
to be reques- the way
they expect to be treated,

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:46.470
and your developers will--

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:49.050
you will frequently experience
developer abandonment,

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:51.030
because your developers
will struggle

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:53.370
to adopt your GraphQL API.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:55.980
So avoid the GraphQL
antipatterns.

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.620
Treat the graph as a
data-driven hierarchy.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:09:59.910
Don't force GraphQL.

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:02.310
Use REST where and
when it makes sense.

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:04.650
And lastly, optimized
for re-usability.

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:05.928
Think about API products.

00:10:05.928 --> 00:10:07.470
Think about the
developer experience,

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:09.760
and treating the
developer as the customer

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:13.260
they are, because you want
your GraphQL API to be used,

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:16.880
and you want it
to get to market.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:18.968
There are challenges
in GraphQL deployments,

00:10:18.968 --> 00:10:21.260
and I'm going to go over
these challenges very quickly,

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:23.510
because I think we're all
somewhat familiar with them.

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:25.400
AuthZ, authorization
authentication

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:26.630
is a huge challenge.

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:28.490
As you know, the
GraphQL spec delegates

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:30.930
AuthZ to the
business logic layer,

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:32.960
which means that it's
typically not handled

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:34.640
by the GraphQL server.

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:35.850
Throttling is a challenge.

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:38.720
There's no standard, like we
see an API productization,

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:42.260
for creating different levels
and different tiers of service,

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:45.440
and different access to
different APIs developers.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:46.500
The third is analytics.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.140
There's no unified
view of analytics,

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:51.870
which means leveraging APIs
as products, raising APIs

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:53.870
to the level of
business analytics,

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:55.540
as opposed to just
operational metrics,

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:57.570
is not something
that's possible.

00:10:57.570 --> 00:10:59.480
And lastly, versioning
ops, we don't

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:02.120
understand how versioning
works in GraphQL,

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:05.445
and how particularly to address
the need for clients using

00:11:05.445 --> 00:11:07.070
different versions
of different schemas

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:10.410
in order to implement
their applications.

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:12.290
So now you know what to do.

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:13.820
How do you do it?

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:16.850
For that, I'd like to
introduce Miguel Apigee is

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:19.190
a highly extensible and
customizable platform

00:11:19.190 --> 00:11:21.063
with great out of
the box features.

00:11:21.063 --> 00:11:22.980
But there are ways to
do other things with it.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:24.810
There's FHIR support
for healthcare,

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:27.740
there's OpenBanking Support for
financial services companies,

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:30.560
and much more,
including GraphQL.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:32.910
With that, I'd like to
hand it over to Miguel.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:35.240
Miguel, take it away.

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:36.740
MIGUEL MENDOZA: All
right, so first,

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:39.200
let's take a look at an
overview of the architecture

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:40.970
for this demo.

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:43.820
On the left side, we have
the Apigee developer portal,

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:47.870
and in this developer portal,
we have the GraphQL Playground

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.530
embedded within the
Apigee developer portal.

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:52.760
Next, we have the Apigee
API gateway, which

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:55.040
is fronting Apollo GraphQL.

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:58.730
The Apollo GraphQL server
is running in Cloud Run.

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:00.350
Within the Apollo
GraphQL server,

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:02.360
there are also a couple
of Apigee plugins

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:05.272
that send logging and
tracing data to GCP.

00:12:05.272 --> 00:12:06.980
Throughout this demo,
we're going to dive

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:09.870
into each of these components,
left to right, one at a time.

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:11.250
Let's go ahead and get started.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:13.580
First let's go and take a
look at the developer portal.

00:12:18.165 --> 00:12:20.040
I already have navigated
to developer portal,

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:21.840
so let me go to that.

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:23.490
Here, in the
developer portal, this

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:26.220
is an Apigee Drupal
developer portal

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:28.602
that has the GraphQL
Playground embedded.

00:12:28.602 --> 00:12:30.060
As you can see
here, the Playground

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:32.110
has already been loaded
with an endpoint.

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:34.110
This endpoint is
the Apigee endpoint

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:36.540
that frames the
Apollo GraphQL server.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:38.730
We can take a quick
look at the docs tab,

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:40.710
and we'll see the
schema that has been

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:42.630
loaded into the Playground.

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:44.290
All right, let's
take a look at it.

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:46.950
So this schema
represents a company

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:50.820
that aggregates trail and
lift data for ski resorts.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:54.570
You can see the top level, we
have a ski resort data type,

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:56.730
and within the ski
resort type, we

00:12:56.730 --> 00:12:59.160
have differing information
and some of that information

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:01.960
needs trails and lift data.

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:07.200
Let me show you a quick
diagram that shows this schema.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:09.560
You can see here, the resort
type, and then under that,

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:11.370
we have trails and lift data.

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:14.060
This is the reading,
or the query schema.

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:15.747
There's also a
mutation schema, but I

00:13:15.747 --> 00:13:17.580
won't show that yet,
we'll go to that later.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:20.570
So let's head back into
the developer portal.

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:24.750
Let's run a query that list all
the resorts that are available.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:30.780
So we do a query, list all
resorts, and for each resort,

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:32.750
let's just pull the
name of the resort.

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:34.670
And we can run this
query, and you can

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:36.170
see that we have three resorts.

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:38.018
Kirkwood Sierra at
Tahoe, and Heavenly.

00:13:38.018 --> 00:13:40.310
Now, if I keep trying to run
this query multiple times,

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:42.240
let's see what happens.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.550
You see, we had an error.

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:45.980
So we have a rate limiter.

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:50.410
This is Apigee that has
detected that the client has

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:52.830
sent too many requests over
a certain period of time.

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:55.440
So within Apigee, it's
running a quota policy,

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.350
and that policy
rejected the call.

00:13:57.350 --> 00:13:58.880
So, how do we get around this?

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:02.090
One way to get around this is
to actually get an API key,

00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:04.340
and maybe make a call
using a specific API

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:07.220
key that has a higher
entitlement, in terms

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:08.490
of calls per minute.

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:09.200
So let's do that.

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:10.850
Notice I'm not even logged in.

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:13.490
Apigee rejected a call based
on the client IP address,

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:15.810
and the number of calls
originating from that address.

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:18.185
So let's go ahead and log in
so that we can create an app

00:14:18.185 --> 00:14:19.013
and get an API key

00:14:19.013 --> 00:14:21.680
I already have an account that I
created earlier. so let me just

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:22.763
looking with that account.

00:14:24.918 --> 00:14:26.460
Now let's go ahead
and create an app.

00:14:29.330 --> 00:14:33.520
I'm going to call
my app peter-app.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:36.350
And for this app, I'm going
to select a GraphQL Bronze API

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:36.890
product.

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:39.050
This product has a
higher entitlement,

00:14:39.050 --> 00:14:40.550
so that I will be
able to make calls

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:42.860
without being worried so much
about the number of calls

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:43.360
per minute.

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:49.370
Right, so my app
has been created.

00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:52.120
Now I should be able to get
the API key from this app.

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:55.540
Let's grab the API key
and secret for this app,

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:57.760
So here, we have the new
app that was created,

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:00.220
and we'll grab the API
key, or the consumer

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:02.110
key and the consumer secret.

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:04.360
I'm going to open the GraphQL
Playground in a new tab.

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:06.568
That way, I can just copy
paste as values over there.

00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:14.657
So let me copy the consumer key.

00:15:14.657 --> 00:15:16.740
All right, so back here
in the GraphQL Playground,

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:18.900
let's use the consumer key
and the consumer secret.

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:21.900
So in Apigee, we have
modified a GraphQL Playground

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:24.640
slightly to make this experience
a little bit smoother.

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:26.040
So on the top
right here, you can

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:27.510
see client credentials button.

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:30.300
This is a widget that
we have added expressly

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:31.420
for this purpose.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:36.450
So let's click on here, and
let's add the client ID.

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:38.190
Right, so we have client ID.

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:43.240
Next, let's grab the client
secret, or the consumer secret.

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:46.570
And let's go back in here and
paste the consumer secret.

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:51.040
And you can also see the scopes
are going to be requested.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:53.770
Basically this is a
proper Auth Flutter

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:55.040
that we're going to be doing.

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:58.060
And we're going to be requesting
a token that has introspection

00:15:58.060 --> 00:15:58.810
and reading--

00:15:58.810 --> 00:15:59.770
reader scopes.

00:15:59.770 --> 00:16:00.534
Let's get a token.

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:03.690
Right, great.

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:04.800
So we have a token.

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:07.658
So this token came
from Apigee itself.

00:16:07.658 --> 00:16:09.450
And we can take a look
at the token itself.

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:11.790
And you can see there's some
details about the token.

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:14.935
And we have the reader scope.

00:16:14.935 --> 00:16:16.310
So let's go ahead
and close this.

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:19.580
Now, we should be able to
run this query multiple times

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:22.677
with this token that we
just got using the API key.

00:16:22.677 --> 00:16:23.260
Let's do that.

00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:28.780
And you can see now that
we're not hitting this limit

00:16:28.780 --> 00:16:30.750
that we were hitting earlier.

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:32.200
Cool.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:33.950
We're not getting
that error anymore.

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:36.280
So you can see that
since we got the API key,

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:38.650
and we were able to get a
token with the proper scope,

00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:39.970
that error is going away.

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:41.678
What if we want to do
something else now?

00:16:41.678 --> 00:16:45.490
Let's say we want to add a
resort to the current data set.

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:49.280
Let's take a look at the
schema for the mutations.

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:52.420
So if we look at mutations,
we have a resource mutation.

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:54.440
And if we want to create
a resort in this case,

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:57.610
this follows more like almost
like REST crowd example.

00:16:57.610 --> 00:16:59.830
Create, delete, update.

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:02.930
So intuitively, to
create a resort,

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:06.339
we will go here into the
create resort mutation.

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:08.710
And you can see that the
create resort mutation

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:10.670
takes a create resort input.

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:12.920
And this is the data that
we're going to be passing in

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:14.079
to create a new resort.

00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:16.329
So let's go ahead and do this.

00:17:16.329 --> 00:17:17.260
One more thing.

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:19.119
Notice that-- it's
documented here

00:17:19.119 --> 00:17:21.849
that in order to run
the resort mutation,

00:17:21.849 --> 00:17:23.440
you need a site admin scope.

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:26.910
You'll see how that comes
into play in a second.

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:30.050
So let's try to run a mutation
for creating a resort.

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:37.810
Create new resort.

00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:40.710
And this is going to
be the resort mutation.

00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:43.240
And under the resort mutation,
we're going to go on create.

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:49.160
And for create, we're
going to pass an input,

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:51.860
and this input we're going to
give the name of the resort,

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:55.030
let's say this is
going to be Northstar.

00:17:55.030 --> 00:18:02.070
And the base elevation,
let's say, 6,300 feet.

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:07.080
And the summit
elevation, 8,500 feet.

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:09.840
After we create a
new resort, let's

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:12.400
pull up the ID of the
resort which was created

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:15.007
and the name of the resort.

00:18:15.007 --> 00:18:17.340
All right, so should be good,
let's try to run this now.

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:21.090
Oh, we got an error.

00:18:21.090 --> 00:18:23.870
So again, this is Apigee
returning the error.

00:18:23.870 --> 00:18:26.180
This is not coming from
the backend GraphQL server.

00:18:26.180 --> 00:18:27.555
Apigee is saying,
hey, you're not

00:18:27.555 --> 00:18:28.940
authorized to create a resort.

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:30.980
You don't have access
to these fields,

00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:35.000
so you cannot access
the resort's mutation

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:37.220
and the resort mutation create.

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:38.660
So these fields
are not authorized

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:41.300
with the current key, or the
current token that I have.

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:45.700
So how do we make this work?

00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:48.350
Obviously the token
that we have right now,

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:50.440
let's take a look at
it again, this token,

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:53.680
it only has these two scopes,
introspection and radar scopes.

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:57.460
And in order to be able
to run this mutation,

00:18:57.460 --> 00:19:01.400
according to the mutation, we
need this site admin scope.

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:04.010
So in order to get a token
that has the site admin scope,

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:07.690
we need to have access to an API
product that offers that scope.

00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:09.130
Or that offers those scopes.

00:19:09.130 --> 00:19:12.110
So let's take a look
back at the API products.

00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:15.460
So if we go back into the
app that we created earlier,

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:17.680
this app subscribes to the
GraphQL Bronze product.

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:21.490
This GraphQL Bronze product does
not offer the site admin scope.

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:23.515
In order to get this
site admin scope,

00:19:23.515 --> 00:19:24.890
I need access to
another product.

00:19:24.890 --> 00:19:27.140
So I have already created
another app called shredder,

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:29.890
and this app has access
to GraphQL Platinum.

00:19:29.890 --> 00:19:32.200
So the GraphQL Platinum
product gives us

00:19:32.200 --> 00:19:33.940
a higher level of
access, in terms

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:36.800
of different part of the
schema that we can access.

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:39.910
And this product has
already been approved,

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:41.180
and it's enabled for this app.

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:46.650
So let's switch to use the key
and secret for this other app.

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:49.950
Right let's grab the consumer
key and bring it over here.

00:19:53.145 --> 00:19:54.270
Next, let's get the secret.

00:19:57.950 --> 00:20:01.765
And finally let's change
the scope to site admin.

00:20:01.765 --> 00:20:03.140
So this scope will
give us access

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:05.600
to a different
part of the schema.

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:07.310
Now let's get a token.

00:20:07.310 --> 00:20:08.503
And we have a new token.

00:20:08.503 --> 00:20:09.920
Hopefully now with
this new token,

00:20:09.920 --> 00:20:12.337
we should be able to make the
call to create a new resort.

00:20:12.337 --> 00:20:13.823
Let's do that.

00:20:13.823 --> 00:20:15.740
Great, and you can see
the call is successful.

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:18.260
And we can verify that it
was successful by listing

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:19.317
all the resorts again.

00:20:19.317 --> 00:20:19.900
Let's do that.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:26.770
Right so we get all
resorts, let's get

00:20:26.770 --> 00:20:28.600
the names of all the resorts.

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:32.350
And you can see here,
Northstar has been created.

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:35.120
All right, so you have seen two
important key functionalities

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:38.360
that Apigee is adding on top of
the existing GraphQL backend.

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:40.340
First, you saw
throttling, and then, you

00:20:40.340 --> 00:20:42.805
saw field-level authorization.

00:20:42.805 --> 00:20:44.180
Now let's peer
behind the curtain

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:46.550
to see how that is
done within Apigee.

00:20:46.550 --> 00:20:49.220
Let me bring up the architecture
diagram, just to remind you

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:49.790
where we are.

00:20:49.790 --> 00:20:52.280
So we're looking so far
at the developer portal.

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:54.650
Next, we're going to look
at the Apigee API gateway

00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:57.710
to see how those throttling
and field-level authorization

00:20:57.710 --> 00:21:00.017
were actually happening.

00:21:00.017 --> 00:21:01.850
All right, let's head
over to the Apigee UI.

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:05.310
Great.

00:21:05.310 --> 00:21:07.560
So here, this is
a proxy that was

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:10.560
driving the entire experience
you're seen so far.

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:12.360
The first policies
that you can see here

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:14.720
are coarse level
authorization policies.

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:16.140
It's just saying,
hey, do you even

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:20.670
have a valid token that
allows you to call this API?

00:21:20.670 --> 00:21:23.430
Then we have the
throttling policy,

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:25.290
or the quota
policy, that checks,

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:27.090
are you allowed to make
the number of calls

00:21:27.090 --> 00:21:28.610
you've been making so far?

00:21:28.610 --> 00:21:30.750
Finally, we have the
fine-grained authorization

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:35.400
policy that checks whether
or not the current token has

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:37.710
the correct level of
access for the fields

00:21:37.710 --> 00:21:41.040
within the specific query,
or the specific mutation

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:43.660
that's being sent to
the GraphQL server.

00:21:43.660 --> 00:21:47.060
Let's take a look
at the quota policy.

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:49.700
You can see this policy has
been configured with a default

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:52.260
value of five calls per minute.

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:54.260
This is, in fact, the
behavior you saw initially

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:56.010
when I was making those
first calls to get

00:21:56.010 --> 00:21:57.120
the list of resorts.

00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:00.110
, Later once I created an
app and subscribed my app

00:22:00.110 --> 00:22:03.380
to the Bronze, or the
GraphQL Bronze API product,

00:22:03.380 --> 00:22:05.210
I was able to make more calls.

00:22:05.210 --> 00:22:08.750
And that's defined outside,
that's defined in the product

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:09.510
itself.

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:12.680
So if we look at the
GraphQL Bronze product,

00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:14.300
you can see that
the GraphQL Bronze

00:22:14.300 --> 00:22:19.440
product has defined a quota
of 30 requests per minute.

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:21.710
Next, let's look at how the
fine-grain authorization

00:22:21.710 --> 00:22:22.960
was working.

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:25.520
Here, you can see in the
GraphQL Bronze product

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:29.000
that I have defined a set
of allowed Auth scopes.

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:32.050
These are introspection
and reader scope.

00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:33.550
I also have the
other product, which

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:38.150
was the GraphQL Platinum, which
has an even bigger set of Auth

00:22:38.150 --> 00:22:38.720
scopes.

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:40.880
In addition to introspection
and reader scope,

00:22:40.880 --> 00:22:44.030
it's got resort admin, resort
manager, and site admin.

00:22:44.030 --> 00:22:45.770
Remember, we used
site admin when

00:22:45.770 --> 00:22:49.960
we went when we went to
create the new resort.

00:22:49.960 --> 00:22:50.848
So this is great.

00:22:50.848 --> 00:22:52.390
You can see how
you're able to manage

00:22:52.390 --> 00:22:56.920
both the SLA, the throttling
level, and the fine grain

00:22:56.920 --> 00:22:59.290
authorization to the
GraphQL endpoint,

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:02.770
all within Apigee using the
concept of API products.

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:05.110
Now you may be asking
yourself, how are these scopes

00:23:05.110 --> 00:23:09.200
being translated to individual
fields in the GraphQL schema?

00:23:09.200 --> 00:23:10.840
Let's take a look at that next.

00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:12.640
All right, so how do
we go from a scope

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:15.670
to an actual set of
fields, or an entitlement?

00:23:15.670 --> 00:23:18.220
To do that, we have
stored the configuration

00:23:18.220 --> 00:23:21.677
of a mapping of scopes to
fields in Apigee itself.

00:23:21.677 --> 00:23:22.760
Let's take a look at that.

00:23:22.760 --> 00:23:25.970
This is in Apigee
KVM, key value map.

00:23:25.970 --> 00:23:29.620
So this is starting here, and--
let me open it so you can see.

00:23:29.620 --> 00:23:33.010
For each scope, I
have listed here

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:35.800
the entitlement or
the set of fields

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:37.930
that this code is
allowed to access.

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:39.750
And in the case
of earlier, where

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:41.560
we are using this
site admin scope,

00:23:41.560 --> 00:23:44.590
and you can see the site admin
has access to mutation resorts,

00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:46.750
and mutation resorts wildcard.

00:23:46.750 --> 00:23:49.090
Which means that if you
had this site admin scope,

00:23:49.090 --> 00:23:52.810
you're token is allowed to
access any of these types

00:23:52.810 --> 00:23:55.165
within the GraphQL schema.

00:23:55.165 --> 00:23:56.790
All right so let's
go back to the proxy

00:23:56.790 --> 00:23:59.428
and put it all together.

00:23:59.428 --> 00:24:01.720
The first policy that runs
is the authorization policy.

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:03.660
This is coarse
level authorization.

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:05.670
It just checks hey,
do you have a token?

00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:09.752
And once you have that token,
it extracts basically the scopes

00:24:09.752 --> 00:24:11.460
that you have associated
with that token.

00:24:11.460 --> 00:24:13.252
It also verifies that
this token is allowed

00:24:13.252 --> 00:24:14.922
to access the proxy itself.

00:24:14.922 --> 00:24:16.380
It doesn't check
individual fields,

00:24:16.380 --> 00:24:18.690
but it extracts the scopes.

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:21.190
Then you have the quota
policy that checks, hey,

00:24:21.190 --> 00:24:23.670
you are you still within
your allowed limit?

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:26.670
And then you have the fine
grain authorization policy.

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:28.860
For this fine grain
authorization policy,

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:31.320
it basically takes
the scope that

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:33.990
was associated with the token
that came in, and converts

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:35.880
that scope to a set
of entitlements,

00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:38.040
or a set of fields,
and it checks

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:41.370
that the set of entitlements,
or a set of fields

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:46.080
that are allowed for this token,
satisfy all the fields that

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:49.080
came in in this specific query
or the specific mutation.

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:50.820
And if that's the
case, then Apigee

00:24:50.820 --> 00:24:52.620
will go ahead and
let the call through,

00:24:52.620 --> 00:24:54.200
and go through the
GraphQL backend.

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:56.635
And if not, then Apigee
returns an error.

00:24:56.635 --> 00:24:58.260
Next, let's take a
look at what happens

00:24:58.260 --> 00:25:00.630
after Apigee send a call
over to the actual GraphQL

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:02.320
backend server.

00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:06.800
Let me bring up the
architecture diagram again.

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:09.140
The call came in from the
developer portal, arrived

00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:12.350
at the Apigee API gateway,
Apigee checked the call

00:25:12.350 --> 00:25:16.310
for security, coarse level
and fine-grain field level

00:25:16.310 --> 00:25:17.120
authorization.

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:21.050
It also did throttling checks,
and then it sent the call over

00:25:21.050 --> 00:25:23.610
to the actual Apollo
GraphQL server.

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:27.050
This server, I have it
running in GCP, in Cloud Run.

00:25:27.050 --> 00:25:29.900
And within here, I
have the Apollo server.

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:31.970
I have added a couple
of Apigee plugins

00:25:31.970 --> 00:25:35.750
that allow you to send data
over to GCP cloud login,

00:25:35.750 --> 00:25:37.880
and also to cloud tracing.

00:25:37.880 --> 00:25:41.120
And this data is sent
asynchronously in batches.

00:25:41.120 --> 00:25:44.270
So it does not affect the
runtime of the main call

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:44.953
itself.

00:25:44.953 --> 00:25:46.370
Let's hop over to
the Google Cloud

00:25:46.370 --> 00:25:49.650
console and take a look
at some of this data.

00:25:49.650 --> 00:25:52.020
Here, I have the GCP
log viewer, and I'm

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:55.710
showing the create new resort
mutation that we ran earlier.

00:25:55.710 --> 00:25:57.960
You can see the actual payload
for the mutation that's

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:01.020
being logged, as well as a
timestamp for this mutation.

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:03.520
Let's go over to the trace.

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:06.570
So here, each of these dots
represent an individual query

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:10.200
or mutation that has been sent
to the Apollo GraphQL server.

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:11.790
And I have selected
already here,

00:26:11.790 --> 00:26:15.240
the mutation that was run to
create the Northstar resort.

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:17.040
You can see the trace
information shows

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:20.070
how long it took to fetch
each individual field

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:21.220
in that mutation.

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:23.730
This is all data that is
produced by Apollo itself,

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:25.860
and is being shipped
off to Google Cloud,

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:28.300
and shown here in
the trace view.

00:26:28.300 --> 00:26:31.290
So this gives you a good
view of the individual timing

00:26:31.290 --> 00:26:33.840
and tracing information
for specific queries.

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:36.240
What if you want more
trend information,

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:39.210
or over time information
of the data itself?

00:26:39.210 --> 00:26:41.790
Apigee collects
analytics information

00:26:41.790 --> 00:26:44.080
for every request that
comes through the system.

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:46.122
So let's go over and take
a look at some of that.

00:26:48.620 --> 00:26:50.280
Let me switch to
Apigee analytics.

00:26:50.280 --> 00:26:53.520
So here, I have created
a custom report in Apigee

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:56.220
that takes a look at the
individual fields that

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:59.460
were fetched for
queries over time,

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:02.460
and also mutations as they
passed through the Apigee

00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:03.330
gateway.

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:06.510
This data has been enhanced with
the timing information provided

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:07.630
by Apollo itself.

00:27:07.630 --> 00:27:10.140
You can see that it
shows different fields,

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:12.810
how many times each of those
fields have been fetched,

00:27:12.810 --> 00:27:14.700
and the average
response time that it

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:17.197
takes to retrieve those fields.

00:27:17.197 --> 00:27:19.530
You can also create all types
of reports with this data,

00:27:19.530 --> 00:27:21.100
it's not just limited to fields.

00:27:21.100 --> 00:27:23.300
You can do different
aggregations, maybe per client,

00:27:23.300 --> 00:27:26.907
and which clients are
requesting what kinds of fields.

00:27:26.907 --> 00:27:28.740
In fact, let's drill
down into one of these.

00:27:32.730 --> 00:27:35.870
So you can see here that the
differing apps, for example,

00:27:35.870 --> 00:27:38.030
a product Playground
app, is requesting

00:27:38.030 --> 00:27:41.450
some traffic over here
and the peter-app, which

00:27:41.450 --> 00:27:44.600
is the one I created, also has
some traffic for the resorts

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:45.325
name.

00:27:45.325 --> 00:27:46.700
Hopefully, this
gives you an idea

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:50.000
of how powerful both
combining Apigee analytics

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:52.970
with the cloud tracing
data and cloud logging data

00:27:52.970 --> 00:27:55.640
can be to enhance the
information that you

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:58.970
can get for the traffic going
through your GraphQL server.

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:00.620
Well folks, that's
it for the demo.

00:28:00.620 --> 00:28:02.460
Now let me hand it
back to you, David,

00:28:02.460 --> 00:28:04.043
DAVID FEUER: That
was awesome, Miguel.

00:28:04.043 --> 00:28:04.910
Thank you.

00:28:04.910 --> 00:28:09.080
So what are three things
you can do today GraphQL?

00:28:09.080 --> 00:28:11.270
If you use Apigee
to manage your APIs,

00:28:11.270 --> 00:28:13.680
you're one step closer
to your destination.

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:16.920
Go download the GraphQL GitHub
sample and play with it.

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:19.200
We'd love to hear your feedback.

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:22.730
If you're not an Apigee user,
go to apigee.com, try out

00:28:22.730 --> 00:28:25.970
the free version of Apigee,
use GraphQL or don't, and do

00:28:25.970 --> 00:28:26.600
cool stuff.

00:28:26.600 --> 00:28:28.650
We'd love to hear
about that as well.

00:28:28.650 --> 00:28:30.650
And lastly, if
you're a GraphQL user

00:28:30.650 --> 00:28:33.470
and you want to learn about
the best place to build APIs,

00:28:33.470 --> 00:28:36.190
Google Cloud's Cloud Run
is an awesome backend

00:28:36.190 --> 00:28:38.300
for your GraphQL
serverless applications.

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:40.570
Definitely check it out.

00:28:40.570 --> 00:28:42.290
Thank you so much
for your time today.

00:28:42.290 --> 00:28:44.373
We look forward to seeing
